@note As a prerequisite of this program, you need to have both PETSc and the
p4est library installed. The installation of deal.II
together with these two additional libraries is described in the <a
href="../../readme.html" target="body">README</a> file.

<a name="step_106.Intro"></a>

<h1>Introduction</h1>

<h3>Navier Stokes equations with Lagrange multiplier</h3>

In this tutorial, we are interested in solving the unsteady incompressible Navier-Stokes
equations in a domain \f$\Omega \subset \mathbb{R}^{d}, d=2,3 \f$.
Instead of enforcing no-slip boundary conditions as Dirichlet conditions as usual,
we would instead like to enforce these constraints weakly, using a Lagrange multiplier
defined on the relevant boundaries. By doing so, the Lagrange multiplier is identified to the flux 
of the associated variable, which for the Stokes or Navier-Stokes equations allows
to recover the fluid force on those boundaries.

We consider the Navier-Stokes system, and the problem of finding 
a velocity \f$\textbf{u}(\textbf{x}, t)\f$ and a pressure \f$\textbf{p}(\textbf{x}, t)\f$ fields
satisfying
@f{align}{
  \rho \left(\partial_t\textbf{u} + (\textbf{u} \cdot \nabla)\textbf{u} + \textbf{f}\right)
  - \nabla \cdot \boldsymbol{\sigma}(p, \mathbf{u})
  %+ \nabla p - \nu (\nabla\textbf{u} + (\nabla\textbf{u})^T)
  &= 0\quad \text{in } \Omega, \\
  -\nabla \cdot \mathbf{u} &= 0 \quad \text{in } \Omega, \\
  \mathbf{u} &= \mathbf{g} \quad \text{on } \Gamma_D,\\
  \boldsymbol{\sigma}(p, \mathbf{u}) &= -p\mathbf{I} + \mu (\nabla\textbf{u} + (\nabla\textbf{u})^T),
@f}
together with suitable boundary conditions on \f$\Gamma_N := \partial\Omega / \Gamma_D\f$,
and an initial velocity \f$\mathbf{u}(\mathbf{x},0) = \mathbf{u}_0(\mathbf{x})\f$.
To derive the weak formulation,
we multiply the momentum equation by a velocity test function \f$\mathbf{v}\f$
and the mass equation by a pressure test function \f$q\f$,
integrate over the domain,
and write, after integrating by parts the stress terms:
@f{align}{
  \left(\mathbf{v}, \rho\left[\partial_t\textbf{u} + (\textbf{u} \cdot \nabla)\textbf{u}
  + \textbf{f}\right]\right)_\Omega
  %- (\nabla \cdot \mathbf{v}, p) - \nu (\nabla\textbf{u} + (\nabla\textbf{u})^T)
  + (\nabla \mathbf{v}, \boldsymbol{\sigma})_\Omega
  - (\mathbf{v}, \boldsymbol{\sigma} \cdot \hat{\boldsymbol{n}})_{\partial\Omega}
  &= 0, \\
  -(q, \nabla \cdot \mathbf{u})_\Omega &= 0.
@f}
When the Dirichlet condition \f$\mathbf{u} = \mathbf{g}\f$ is strongly enforced,
the test function \f$\mathbf{v}\f$ is zero on the associated boundary,
and the boundary term \f$(\mathbf{v}, \boldsymbol{\sigma} \cdot \hat{\boldsymbol{n}})_\Omega\f$
reduces to \f$(\mathbf{v}, \boldsymbol{\sigma} \cdot \hat{\boldsymbol{n}})_{\Gamma_N}\f$,
on the parts of the boundary where a Neumann condition is applied.
In the Lagrange multiplier framework,
the boundary condition \f$\mathbf{u} = \mathbf{g}\f$ is a constraint that becomes
a part of the system of PDEs, and we introduce on \f$\Gamma_D\f$ an
auxiliary variable \f$\boldsymbol{\lambda}(\mathbf{x},t)\f$, whose role is to enforce this constraint.
The constraint equation is then also multiplied by the Lagrange multiplier test function
\f$\hat{\boldsymbol{\lambda}}\f$, and 
the modified system takes the form

@f{align}{
  \left(\mathbf{v}, \rho\left[\partial_t\textbf{u} + (\textbf{u} \cdot \nabla)\textbf{u} + \textbf{f}\right]\right)_\Omega
  %- (\nabla \cdot \mathbf{v}, p) - \nu (\nabla\textbf{u} + (\nabla\textbf{u})^T)
  + (\nabla \mathbf{v}, \boldsymbol{\sigma})_\Omega
  - (\mathbf{v}, \boldsymbol{\sigma} \cdot \hat{\boldsymbol{n}})_{\Gamma_N}
  - (\mathbf{v}, \boldsymbol{\lambda})_{\Gamma_D}
  &= 0, \\
  -(q, \nabla \cdot \mathbf{u})_\Omega &= 0, \\
  - (\hat{\boldsymbol{\lambda}}, \mathbf{u} - \mathbf{g})_{\Gamma_D} &= 0.\\
@f}

By introducing \f$\boldsymbol{\lambda}\f$ this way,
it is identified to the stress vector \f$\boldsymbol{t}(\hat{\mathbf{n}}) := \boldsymbol{\sigma} \cdot \hat{\mathbf{n}}\f$
on the fluid from the cylinder, which is up to sign the stress vector on the cylinder from
the fluid, since their normal vectors are the opposite of one another.
This provides a handy way of postprocessing the fluid forces on boundaries where
no-slip condition are enforced this way, as we can simply take the integral
of the Lagrange multiplier required to satisfy this constraint.
This is, of course, at the cost of an extra variable on \f$\Gamma_D\f$ to handle and solve for.

@note In other tutorial steps solving the incompressible (Navier-)Stokes equations (e.g., step 57),
the divergence of the viscous term
\f$\mu \nabla \cdot (\nabla \mathbf{u} + (\nabla \mathbf{u})^T) = \mu (\Delta \mathbf{u} + \nabla(\nabla \cdot \mathbf{u}))\f$
is usually simplified to \f$\mu \Delta \mathbf{u}\f$, taking advantage of the incompressibility constraint.
Here, however, both terms must be kept if we want to identify \f$\boldsymbol{\lambda}\f$
to the full stress vector, and obtain the complete fluid forces.

<h3>Newton-Raphson method</h3>

The system above can be recast into finding the triplet \f$U := (\mathbf{u}, p, \boldsymbol{\lambda})\f$
such that the following nonlinear functional vanishes:
@f{equation}{
\text{NL}(U) :=
\left(\mathbf{v}, \rho\left[\partial_t\textbf{u} + (\textbf{u} \cdot \nabla)\textbf{u} + \textbf{f}\right]\right)_\Omega
  + (\nabla \mathbf{v}, \boldsymbol{\sigma})_\Omega
  - (\mathbf{v}, \boldsymbol{\sigma} \cdot \hat{\boldsymbol{n}})_{\Gamma_N}
  - (\mathbf{v}, \boldsymbol{\lambda})_{\Gamma_D}
  -(q, \nabla \cdot \mathbf{u})_\Omega
  - (\hat{\boldsymbol{\lambda}}, \mathbf{u} - \mathbf{g})_{\Gamma_D} = 0.
\f}

This problem is solved using Newton-Raphson's method,
which determines at each time step the solution \f$U^n\f$ satisfying
\f$\text{NL}(U^n) = 0\f$ up to a prescribed tolerance.
This is done, as in step 57, by computing a sequence of increments \f$\Delta U^n_k\f$, obtained by solving
at each iteration the linear system

@f{equation}{
J(U^n_k) \Delta U^n_k = -\text{NL}(U^n_k),
\f}

where \f$J(U^n_k)\f$ is the Jacobian matrix of \f$\text{NL}\f$ evaluated at the current solution.
Since the Jacobian matrix is easy to obtain for the system of interest,
we use in this tutorial the exact Jacobian, but automatic differentiation tools exist to
compute an approximation of the matrix when it becomes too cumbersome to compute (see e.g. step 72).
The exact Jacobian matrix is typically computed by discretizing the Gateaux derivative of \f$\text{NL}(U)\f$
in the direction of a variation of a component of \f$U\f$, which is essentially the function-valued
directional derivative:

@f{equation}{
J(U) \Delta U := \delta\text{NL}(U, \delta U)
= \frac{d}{d\epsilon}\text{NL}(U + \epsilon \delta U)\biggr\vert_{\epsilon = 0}.
\f}

For instance, the variation of the convective part \f$\rho\left((\textbf{u} \cdot \nabla)\textbf{u}\right)\f$ of \f$\text{NL}(U)\f$ in the direction of a
velocity variation \f$\delta U = (\delta \mathbf{u}, 0, 0)\f$ writes

@f{align}{
\frac{d}{d\epsilon}\text{NL}(U + \epsilon \delta \mathbf{u})\biggr\vert_{\epsilon = 0}
&=
\frac{d}{d\epsilon} \left(\mathbf{v}, \rho \left[((\mathbf{u} + \epsilon \delta \mathbf{u}) \cdot \nabla)
(\mathbf{u} + \epsilon \delta \mathbf{u})\right] \right)\biggr\vert_{\epsilon = 0}\\
&= \left(\mathbf{v}, \rho \left[(\delta \mathbf{u} \cdot \nabla) \mathbf{u} + (\mathbf{u} \cdot \nabla) \delta \mathbf{u}\right] \right),
\f}

where \f$\mathbf{u}\f$ is the known current velocity field, and \f$\delta \mathbf{u}\f$
is the unknown increment we would like to compute.
Introducing the spatial discretization
\f$\delta \mathbf{u}_h = \sum_{j = 0}^{N_\text{dofs}} U_j \boldsymbol{\varphi}_j\f$
and using a continuous Galerkin projection (i.e., \f$\mathbf{v} = \boldsymbol{\varphi}_i\f$),
this yields the convection matrix

@f{align}{
J_{\text{conv}, ij} = \left(\boldsymbol{\varphi}_i, \rho
  \left[(\boldsymbol{\varphi}_j \cdot \nabla) \mathbf{u}_h
    + (\mathbf{u}_h \cdot \nabla) \boldsymbol{\varphi}_j\right] \right),
\f}

Because the convective term is nonlinear, this matrix involves the current velocity field
in addition to the shape functions,,
and thus needs to be updated at each Newton iteration to ensure optimal convergence.
In practice, heuristics can be used to limit its recomputation while maintaining acceptable convergence,
see e.g. the discussion in step XX, but in this tutorial, we will assemble it at each iteration.
All the other terms in \f$\text{NL}(U)\f$ are linear, and the associated Jacobian matrices
thus involve only the shape functions, not the solution itself.

<h3>Implicit time stepping</h3>

To obtain a fully discrete scheme, it remains to approximate the velocity time derivative.
We consider a time discretization using
a backward differentiation formula (BDF) method, which for an order \f$p\f$ method 
uses the solutions at times \f$t_n\f$ (i.e., the current solution) to \f$t_{n-p}\f$:

@f{equation}{
\partial_t\mathbf{u} = \sum_{i = 0}^p c_i \mathbf{u}^{n-c}
\f}

In this tutorial, we will use a simple BDF1 method, a.k.a the implicit Euler scheme, which only requires
the solution at the previous time step.
At time \f$t_n\f$, we thus start the Newton-Raphson loop with the solution at time \f$t_{n-1}\f$ as initial
candidate, and seek the increment \f$\Delta U^n\f$ to obtain \f$U^n = U^{n-1} + \Delta U^n\f$ at convergence.
Then, we postprocess the solution and move on to the next time step.

<h3>Handling the Lagrange multiplier field</h3>

The Lagrange multiplier \f$\boldsymbol{\lambda}\f$ is defined on a boundary (or boundaries) \f$\Gamma_D\f$,
a codimension 1 entity.
The <code>FESystem</code> machinery, however, only allows juxtaposing finite element spaces
of identical dimension.
To keep things simple, a solution is to define \f$\boldsymbol{\lambda}\f$ on the whole domain,
and only use its values on the boundaries of interest, while constraining the remaining degrees of freedom
to an arbitrary, unused value, like zero.
This greatly simplifies the implementation, and avoids to manipulate two DoFHandlers,
at the cost of a whole extra (vector-valued) variable to store and solve for.
When using an iterative solver, these extra constrained dofs are luckily not a huge deal,
but in this tutorial, we will solve the coupled system with a direct solver, which <i>is</i> affected
by the extra dofs.

A more elegant alternative to the current approach is to take advantage of the <i>hp-</i>machinery,
to assign different finite element spaces on cells touching the boundaries of interest,
similarly to what is done in step XX.
This way, we can define an <code>FESystem</code> with the Lagrange multiplier
only on cells with faces on \f$\Gamma_D\f$, and use an <code>FESystem</code>
with an <code>FENothing</code> to represent \f$\boldsymbol{\lambda}\f$ in the rest
of the domain, which does not add any additional unused dofs.
This is mentioned in the "Possibilities for extension" section below.

<h3>Linear solver</h3>

We use the parallel direct solver Mumps to solve the linear system obtained at each Newton iteration.
Thus, the effort put into the linear solves is minimal, but the drawback is that it won't scale well,
and will only allow for problems with a modest number of degrees of freedom, especially in 3D.

<h3>The testcase</h3>

The considered testcase is the well-known incompressible flow around a circular cylinder, in 2D and 3D,
and without source term \f$(\mathbf{f} = 0)\f$.
The computational domain is a cylinder of diameter \f$D\f$
inside a rectangular box of dimensions \f$[(8 + 1 + 24)D, (8 + 1 + 8)D]\f$.
We consider a Newtonian fluid with constant density \f$\rho\f$ and viscosity \f$\mu\f$,
and to simplify, we divide the momentum equation by \f$\rho\f$.
As a result, both the pressure and the Lagrange multiplier are scaled by the density,
and we will need to multiply back by \f$\rho\f$ the integral of the Lagrange multiplier
to evaluate the fluid force on the cylinder.
The Reynolds number of the flow is set to \f$Re := \rho UD/\mu = UD/\nu = 200\f$,
which is achieved by taking \f$U = 1, D = 1, \rho = 1, \nu = 1/Re = 0.005\f$.

On the cylinder, the no-slip boundary condition \f$\mathbf{u} = 0\f$ is weakly enforced,
which amounts to take \f$\mathbf{g} = 0\f$ in the system above.
The remaining boundary conditions are the following:
- On the inlet, the velocity is set to a uniform flow \f$\mathbf{u} = (1, 0)\f$ or \f$\mathbf{u} = (1, 0, 0)\f$,
- On the outlet, we set an outflow (a.k.a a zero traction or do-nothing) boundary condition, which amounts to enforce \f$\boldsymbol{\sigma} \cdot \hat{\mathbf{n}} = 0\f$,
- On the top and bottom planes, we set a slip condition \f$\mathbf{u} \cdot \hat{\mathbf{n}} = 0\f$,
- In 3D, the front and back planes are periodic.

The initial condition for the velocity is the uniform flow, i.e.,
\f$\mathbf{u}_0(\mathbf{x}) = (1,0)\f$ or \f$(1,0,0)\f$.

Since we consider the nonstabilized Navier-Stokes equation,
the choice of discrete spaces for the velocity and pressure is subject to an inf-sup stability
condition, which is satisfied by the Taylor-Hood pair \f$Q^k-Q^{k-1}\f$.
Here, we will simply use the low order pair \f$Q^2-Q^1\f$, but this can easily be modified in the program.
Adding a Lagrange multiplier yields an additional saddle-point type problem,
which comes with its own inf-sup condition on the velocity-lambda discrete pair to ensure
existence and uniqueness of a discrete solution.
This condition, however, is not nearly as well documented, which makes it less straightforward to
select a discrete pair.
In practice, there is no issue with an equal-order approximation in 2D for this benchmark.
This is appealing, because in that case, there is the same number of
velocity and lambda degrees of freedom on the cylinder,
and the no-slip constraints is actually enforced exactly (that is, up to the
precision of the linear solver),
whereas for a Taylor-Hood pair for \f$(\mathbf{u}_h, \boldsymbol{\lambda}_h)\f$,
the constraint enforcement converges with the mesh.
In 3D, we observe oscillations in \f$\boldsymbol{\lambda}_h\f$ when using an equal order pair,
exactly like the checkerboard-like pressure instability when using an equal-order velocity-pressure pair.
We observe, however, that \f$(i)\f$ the no-slip condition is still enforced exactly, and \f$(ii)\f$ the integral of lambda
(i.e., the fluid forces) converges with the expected rate nonetheless.
Thus, the example program below uses an equal-order \f$Q^2\f$ approximation for the Lagrange multiplier by default,
but you should feel free to try different pair combinations.
